WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.460
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily, episode 12.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.580
I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.840
Today is June 23, 2026.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.660
Here's what's happening at SpaceX today.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.700
Starfall's first reentry capsule flight

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.240
tests the hardware path to routine orbital cargo return

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.640
that could enable in-space manufacturing at scale.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.720
The Falcon 9 carried the first Starfall reentry capsule

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.180
on a demonstration flight.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.240
The capsule is designed to return manufactured goods from orbit without crew.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.460
Early data on thermal protection and recovery systems

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.380
will inform follow-on vehicles.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.360
No on-orbit manufacturing payload flew on this initial mission.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.780
The capsule targets point to point return of high-value items produced in microgravity.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.760
Recovery on land or at sea would bypass long-duration station docking constraints.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.160
The design trades payload volume for rapid turnaround compared with dragon.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:06.880
From an engineering standpoint, the compact disk geometry cuts heat shield area

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.340
while still fitting optics or crystals.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:14.100
And by dropping crew systems, it lowers the deorbit propellant load.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.380
If the thermal data hold, the capsule could fly multiple times after only tile inspection.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:25.640
Shrinking the cost multiplier that now separates raw materials from a finished return

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:31.520
vehicle. The next milestone is whether the recovery system can land precisely enough on land

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:38.340
to avoid sea retrieval costs. While Starfall pushes the return leg of orbital manufacturing,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.520
ground infrastructure must scale to match the launch cadence required to feed it.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.720
SpaceX has informed NASA of plans to launch starship every eight days from Kennedy.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.900
Current pad infrastructure, propellant storage, and road networks fall short of that cadence.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.180
The assessment highlights needed upgrades to deluge systems and lightning protection.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.180
No schedule for those upgrades has been released. Kennedy Space Center infrastructure is not yet

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:12.280
sized for the eight-day starship cadence. SpaceX has discussed with NASA.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:18.360
propellant lines, road access, and lightning protection all require upgrades that have no

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:24.680
announced schedule. The gap leaves open the question of whether Florida operations can support the

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:31.020
rapid reuse goals set for the vehicle. Remember the show covered starship flight test cadence on

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:37.000
episode 11. Today's report moves the Florida side of that arc forward by flagging where

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.620
pad limits could slow the reuse goals. Florida upgrades are one piece. Production capacity is the

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:49.640
other half of the same scaling problem. Construction targets an annual output of 1,000 vehicles

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.800
through assembly line methods rather than traditional aerospace hand-built approaches.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:01.420
The facility design emphasizes parallel production of tanks, avionics, and heat shield tiles.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:08.180
Output at that rate would directly support the 15 launch lunar architecture described in separate

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:14.800
reporting. No completion date for the factory is stated. This production push sits squarely in

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:21.060
the starship reuse thread. We have tracked since last episode where the open question remains how

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:27.680
quickly flight rate can climb once vehicles exist. High production volume only matters if the vehicles

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.880
can actually reach the moon. The propellant math shows how many flights that really takes.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.940
The architecture calls for multiple tanker flights to deliver propellant for a single lunar landing

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:45.240
mission. Each tanker must reach orbit, transfer fuel, and return, multiplying total launches.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:52.480
The count assumes current starship payload and specific impulse figures hold. No margin for failed

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.340
refueling attempts is included in the baseline. The same refueling architecture underpins the ongoing

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.780
orbital test campaign. So these numbers directly test whether the 15 flight model can close without

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:12.120
added margin. Domestic test cadence remains the nearer term driver. Flight 13 preparations continue

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:20.360
at both Florida pads. Starship flight 13 prep continues at both Florida pads with 11 deluge system

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:29.920
tests logged so far. LC39A and SLC37A are being ready in parallel for the next integrated flight.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:36.080
The test count reflects emphasis on water flow reliability ahead of higher energy engines.

